[36m```[39m
[36mNew:[39m
[36m```[39m
[36mConfigure Amazon Linux 2023 so the file integrity tool runs automatically on the system at least weekly and notifies designated personnel if baseline configurations are changed in an unauthorized manner. The AIDE tool can be configured to email designated personnel using either the crond service or systemd timers.[39m

[36mIf using cron, ensure the cronie package is installed and enabled:[39m

[36m$ sudo dnf install cronie[39m
[36m$ sudo systemctl enable crond[39m
[36m$ sudo systemctl start crond[39m

[36mThe following example output is generic. It will set cron to run AIDE daily and to send an email upon completion of the analysis:[39m

[36m$ sudo more /etc/cron.daily/aide[39m
[36m#!/bin/bash[39m
[36m/usr/sbin/aide --check | /bin/mail -s "$HOSTNAME - Daily aide integrity check run" root@sysname.mil[39m

[36mIf using systemd timers, create the file "/etc/systemd/system/aid.service" to run the "aide --check" command:[39m

[36m[Unit][39m
[36mDescription=Aide Check[39m
[36m[Service][39m
[36mType=simple[39m
[36mExecStart=/usr/sbin/aide --check | /bin/mail -s "$HOSTNAME - Daily aide integrity check run"[39m
[36m[Install][39m
[36mWantedBy=multi-user.target[39m

[36mCreate the file "/etc/systemd/system/aide.timer" to run the AIDE check service at a specified time. The example below runs "aide --check" at 1:00 a.m. every day:[39m

[36m[Unit][39m
[36mDescription=Aide check every day at 1AM[39m
[36m[Timer][39m
[36mOnCalendar=*-*-* 01:00:00[39m
[36mUnit=aide.service[39m
[36m[Install][39m
[36mWantedBy=timers.target[39m

[36mReload systemd:[39m

[36m$ sudo systemctl daemon-reload[39m

[36mEnabling the service will ensure the "aide --check" service will also start at boot time:[39m

[36m$ sudo systemctl enable aide.service[39m

[36mStart the timer:[39m

[36m$ sudo systemctl --now enable aide.timer[39m

