[36mWithout the use of multifactor authentication, the ease of access to privileged functions is greatly increased.[39m

[36mMultifactor authentication requires using two or more factors to achieve authentication.[39m

[36mFactors include: [39m
[36m1. Something a user knows (e.g., password/PIN);[39m
[36m2. Something a user has (e.g., cryptographic identification device, token); and[39m
[36m3. Something a user is (e.g., biometric).[39m

[36mA privileged account is defined as an information system account with authorizations of a privileged user.[39m

[36mNetwork access is defined as access to an information system by a user (or a process acting on behalf of a user) communicating through a network (e.g., local area network, wide area network, or the internet).[39m

[36mThe DOD Common Access Card (CAC) with DOD-approved PKI is an example of multifactor authentication.[39m



