[36m```[39m
[36mInformation stored in one location is vulnerable to accidental or incidental deletion or alteration.[39m

[36mOff-loading is a common process in information systems with limited audit storage capacity.[39m

[36mRHEL 9 installation media provides "rsyslogd", a system utility providing support for message logging. Support for both internet and Unix domain sockets enables this utility to support both local and remote logging. Coupling this utility with "gnutls" (a secure communications library implementing the SSL, TLS and DTLS protocols) creates a method to securely encrypt and off-load auditing.[39m

[36mRsyslog provides three ways to forward message: the traditional UDP transport, which is extremely lossy but standard; the plain TCP based transport, which loses messages only during certain situations but is widely available; and the RELP transport, which does not lose messages but is currently available only as part of the rsyslogd 3.15.0 and above.[39m

[36mExamples of each configuration:[39m
[36mUDP *.* @remotesystemname[39m
[36mTCP *.* @@remotesystemname[39m
[36mRELP *.* :omrelp:remotesystemname:2514[39m
[36mNote that a port number was given as there is no standard port for RELP.[39m

[36m```[39m
[36mNew:[39m
[36m```[39m
[36mInformation stored in one location is vulnerable to accidental or incidental deletion or alteration.[39m

[36mOff-loading is a common process in information systems with limited audit storage capacity.[39m

[36mSupport for both internet and Unix domain sockets enables this utility to support both local and remote logging. Coupling this utility with "gnutls" (a secure communications library implementing the SSL, TLS, and DTLS protocols) creates a method to securely encrypt and off-load auditing.[39m

